  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1107 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nThe mid/upper level subtropical ridge over the southeast CONUS\nwill merge with ridging over the Desert Southwest by Tuesday. A\ntrough will amplify and deepen over the West Coast early in the\nweek, shifting the ridge axis eastward over the Plains by \nWednesday. This will not only keep the oppressive heat in place, \nbut will also suppress any significant rain chances through at \nleast midweek. With that said, additional heat headlines will \nlikely be needed for Tuesday and Wednesday. By Thursday and \nFriday, the ridge axis pushes farther eastward over the \nMississippi Valley and the southeast CONUS. With the ridge east of\nthe area late in the week, ensembles and medium-range guidance \nsuggests a weak to modest cold front will drift southward from \nthe Northern Plains and into the Central Plains by Thursday into \nThursday afternoon. Model guidance and ensembles stall the front \nand keep it just north of the forecast area through the long- \nterm, but do hint at low probabilities of a few showers and \nthunderstorms, perhaps as a result of outflow boundaries, drifting\ninto the northern parts of the CWA late in the week and into next\nweekend. Assuming this verifies, at the very least, cloud cover \nmay help reduce temperatures slightly by a few degrees for \nportions of the area. Otherwise, oppressive heat is expected to \npersist into next weekend. \n\nMejia",
